        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "gap": "Robustness guarantees and high universal breakdown points for robust k-means on arbitrary datasets without strict geometric constraints",
      "why_it_matters": "Algorithmic safety and outlier resilience depend on robust breakdown limits; however, current robust objective formulations exhibit low universal breakdown points on arbitrary datasets.",
      "what_is_known": "Robust k-means variants using proximal maps or penalized objectives achieve strong consistency and noise robustness under restricted structural assumptions (e.g., well-separated balls containing inliers), but fail on arbitrary datasets where their universal breakdown point drops as low as 2/n.",
      "what_remains_unknown": "Whether alternative penalty structures or objective formulations can fundamentally elevate universal breakdown points for robust k-means on arbitrary, unstructured datasets.",
