          "paper_id": "P005",
          "section": "5",
          "evidence": "The only common characteristic of (RKM) and (TKM) is that they both have the same universal breakdown point, i.e., 2/n, for arbitrary datasets."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "gap": "Integration of robust covariance estimators and streaming concept-drift mechanisms into federated and distributed outlier-aware k-means architectures",
      "why_it_matters": "Distributed and decentralized IoT applications require outlier-aware k-means models to resist poisoned commissioning data and adapt to non-IID distributional shifts without incurring massive communication overheads.",
      "what_is_known": "Adaptive federated baselines use median-absolute-deviation thresholds and benignly anchored aggregations to mitigate merge-induced precision decay, but rely on non-robust sample means and covariances that degrade under contaminated commissioning windows.",
      "what_remains_unknown": "How to incorporate minimum-covariance-determinant (MCD) estimators or robust shrinkage alongside automated streaming drift mechanisms into distributed k-means without violating lightweight serialization and communication constraints.",
      "gap_type": "inferred",
