        "evidence": "On UNSW-NB15, AF-BKM exposes an explicit precision-recall trade-off where lower recall is observed at stricter targets, indicating that secondary local detectors can target ambiguous regions.",
        "evidence_excerpt": "On UNSW-NB15, attacks are harder to separate from benign traffic, and at the low-FPR operating point the MAD rule selects, AF-BKM favors precision and FPR over recall (Table 4). Because the threshold is benignly anchored to a target FPR (Equation (8) with r = τFPR), the operator can choose the trade-off explicitly. Table 7 and Figure 7 sweep the benign target FPR (ϕ ∈ {0.05, 0.10, 0.15, 0.20}): the achieved FPR tracks ϕ, and on UNSW-NB15, recall rises from 0.28 to 0.53 and F1 from 0.40 to 0.60 as ϕ grows from 0.05 to 0.20, while precision falls gently from 0.83 to 0.74. A mid-range ϕ ≈ 0.15–0.20 is a reasonable operating point on UNSW-NB15; on NSL-KDD, the knob spans a recall range of 0.81–0.95, and on N-BaIoT, recall stays at 1.000 for every ϕ, so the knob simply selects the tolerated false-positive level. We therefore frame AF-BKM as a precision/FPR-stability repair with an explicit, controllable operating point, not as a method that maximizes recall on every dataset.",
